  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Biodiverse Landscapes Fund - Lower Mekong</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. Infrastructure and Development 
- In Cambodia and Lao, IUCN will work with Ratanakiri and Xieng Khouang to understand their development plans and provide training and technical design assistance. 
- IUCN will work with ministries of finance and national assemblies in all 3 countries to build political support for infrastructure development that offers VfM and avoids unnecessary social and environment risks. This takes advantage over growing debt risk concerns, particularly in Cambodia and Laos, and more assertive national assemblies, particularly in Vietnam. 
- IUCN will train central and provincial government officials responsible for natural resources, assisting them in designing NbS focused on priority PAs and landscapes. 
- IUCN and SNV will support a MAE initiative to test forest landscape restoration methods in a small number of production forests adjacent to, or connecting, the focal PAs. 

2. Ecotourism 
- The main activity is to replicate successful ecotourism business models, specifically the Oxalis model, with a focus on transboundary learning in Hin Nam No NP (IUCN) and Pu Mat NP (FFI). 
- Capacity building and developing nature-based tourism (low impact/high conservation outcome) in Hin Nam No in partnership with Green Discovery Laos. 
- Promote ecotourism as a tool for green, sustainable post-Covid recovery. 

3. PA management 
- Training needs assessment and capacity building for newly established Yod Nam Mo-Phou Samsoum NPA staff using ASEAN/IUCN PA competency standards 
- Capacity building and support for Yod Nam Mo-Phou Samsoum.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">BLF is a &#xA3;100m UK government program focused on poverty reduction, biodiversity conservation, and climate change in 6 critical landscapes globally. These include the Lower Mekong, covering Cambodia, Lao, and VN.

BLF Lower Mekong will be implemented by a consortium led by FFI with IUCN, SNV, and Re:Wild. IUCN will lead 2 of the 6 components: infrastructure and ecotourism regionally and will lead PA management in Laos.</narrative>
  </description>
